The manager of the System Open Market Account reported on balance sheet developments, noting that holdings of Treasury securities and agency mortgage-backed securities continued to decline under the runoff plan. Redemptions proceeded smoothly, and the pace of reductions remained within the monthly caps announced previously.

Participants discussed conditions in money markets, observing that reserve balances remained ample but that usage of the overnight reverse repurchase facility had declined substantially. Repurchase agreement rates showed occasional pressure around month-end and Treasury settlement dates.

Several participants favored slowing the pace of balance sheet runoff to reduce the risk of disruptions in short-term funding markets. They suggested that a slower pace would allow the Committee to approach ample reserves more gradually and to preserve flexibility.

A few participants preferred to continue runoff at the current pace until reserves approached the level judged consistent with ample conditions. They noted that the standing repo facility could address temporary funding pressures should they arise.

The staff presented analysis of the maturity composition of Treasury holdings and the implications for future redemptions. Participants agreed to continue communicating clearly about the balance sheet plan so that market participants could anticipate changes.

With respect to the policy rate, participants judged that the target range for the federal funds rate should be left unchanged, citing a balanced assessment of risks to the employment and inflation objectives.
